1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,319
Eight o’clock had struck before I got into the air, that was scented, not disagreeably,

2
00:00:11,319 --> 00:00:17,758
by the chips and shavings of the long-shore boat-builders, and mast, oar, and block makers.

3
00:00:17,758 --> 00:00:23,160
All that water-side region of the upper and lower Pool below Bridge was unknown ground to me;

4
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:27,796
and when I struck down by the river, I found that the spot I wanted was not where I had supposed it

5
00:00:27,796 --> 00:00:35,641
to be, and was anything but easy to find. It was called Mill Pond Bank, Millstone Basin;

6
00:00:35,641 --> 00:00:41,559
and I had no other guide to Millstone Basin than the Old Green Copper Rope-walk.

7
00:00:41,559 --> 00:00:47,957
It matters not what stranded ships repairing in dry docks I lost myself among, what old hulls of

8
00:00:47,957 --> 00:00:54,078
ships in course of being knocked to pieces, what ooze and slime and other dregs of tide,

9
00:00:54,078 --> 00:01:00,718
what yards of ship-builders and ship-breakers, what rusty anchors blindly biting into the ground,

10
00:01:01,319 --> 00:01:07,678
though for years off duty, what mountainous country of accumulated casks and timber,

11
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:13,957
how many rope-walks that were not the Old Green Copper. After several times falling short of

12
00:01:13,957 --> 00:01:20,000
my destination and as often overshooting it, I came unexpectedly round a corner,

13
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:27,237
upon Mill Pond Bank. It was a fresh kind of place, all circumstances considered, where the wind from

14
00:01:27,237 --> 00:01:32,519
the river had room to turn itself round; and there were two or three trees in it, and there

15
00:01:32,519 --> 00:01:39,000
was the stump of a ruined windmill, and there was the Old Green Copper Rope-walk,—whose long

16
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:45,038
and narrow vista I could trace in the moonlight, along a series of wooden frames set in the ground,

17
00:01:45,038 --> 00:01:52,718
that looked like superannuated haymaking-rakes which had grown old and lost most of their teeth.

18
00:01:52,718 --> 00:01:57,796
Selecting from the few queer houses upon Mill Pond Bank a house with a wooden front and three stories

19
00:01:57,796 --> 00:02:03,718
of bow-window (not bay-window, which is another thing), I looked at the plate upon the door,

20
00:02:03,718 --> 00:02:08,957
and read there, Mrs. Whimple. That being the name I wanted, I knocked,

21
00:02:08,957 --> 00:02:15,199
and an elderly woman of a pleasant and thriving appearance responded. She was immediately deposed,

22
00:02:15,199 --> 00:02:22,398
however, by Herbert, who silently led me into the parlour and shut the door. It was an odd sensation

23
00:02:22,398 --> 00:02:29,436
to see his very familiar face established quite at home in that very unfamiliar room and region;

24
00:02:29,438 --> 00:02:34,319
and I found myself looking at him, much as I looked at the corner-cupboard with the glass

25
00:02:34,319 --> 00:02:40,678
and china, the shells upon the chimney-piece, and the coloured engravings on the wall, representing

26
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:45,796
the death of Captain Cook, a ship-launch, and his Majesty King George the Third in

27
00:02:45,796 --> 00:02:52,480
a state coachman’s wig, leather-breeches, and top-boots, on the terrace at Windsor.

28
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:57,277
“All is well, Handel,” said Herbert, “and he is quite satisfied, though eager to see

29
00:02:57,277 --> 00:03:02,557
you. My dear girl is with her father; and if you’ll wait till she comes down,

30
00:03:02,559 --> 00:03:08,480
I’ll make you known to her, and then we’ll go upstairs. That’s her father.”

31
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:11,436
I had become aware of an alarming growling overhead,

32
00:03:11,438 --> 00:03:15,438
and had probably expressed the fact in my countenance.

33
00:03:15,438 --> 00:03:20,160
“I am afraid he is a sad old rascal,” said Herbert, smiling,

34
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:25,957
“but I have never seen him. Don’t you smell rum? He is always at it.”

35
00:03:25,957 --> 00:03:28,879
“At rum?” said I.

36
00:03:28,879 --> 00:03:35,879
“Yes,” returned Herbert, “and you may suppose how mild it makes his gout. He persists, too,

37
00:03:35,879 --> 00:03:41,000
in keeping all the provisions upstairs in his room, and serving them out. He keeps them

38
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:47,438
on shelves over his head, and will weigh them all. His room must be like a chandler’s shop.”

39
00:03:47,438 --> 00:03:53,839
While he thus spoke, the growling noise became a prolonged roar, and then died away.

40
00:03:53,839 --> 00:04:00,519
“What else can be the consequence,” said Herbert, in explanation, “if he will cut the cheese?

41
00:04:01,319 --> 00:04:07,199
A man with the gout in his right hand—and everywhere else—can’t expect to get through

42
00:04:07,199 --> 00:04:10,639
a Double Gloucester without hurting himself.”

43
00:04:10,641 --> 00:04:15,160
He seemed to have hurt himself very much, for he gave another furious roar.

44
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:20,078
“To have Provis for an upper lodger is quite a godsend to Mrs. Whimple,” said Herbert,

45
00:04:20,078 --> 00:04:26,000
“for of course people in general won’t stand that noise. A curious place, Handel; isn’t it?”

46
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,436
It was a curious place, indeed; but remarkably well kept and clean.

47
00:04:31,358 --> 00:04:36,838
“Mrs. Whimple,” said Herbert, when I told him so, “is the best of housewives,

48
00:04:36,839 --> 00:04:41,398
and I really do not know what my Clara would do without her motherly help. For,

49
00:04:41,398 --> 00:04:48,436
Clara has no mother of her own, Handel, and no relation in the world but old Gruffandgrim.”

50
00:04:48,438 --> 00:04:50,957
“Surely that’s not his name, Herbert?”

51
00:04:50,957 --> 00:04:57,000
“No, no,” said Herbert, “that’s my name for him. His name is Mr. Barley. But what a blessing it is

52
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,879
for the son of my father and mother to love a girl who has no relations, and who can never bother

53
00:05:01,879 --> 00:05:07,879
herself or anybody else about her family!” Herbert had told me on former occasions,

54
00:05:07,879 --> 00:05:13,957
and now reminded me, that he first knew Miss Clara Barley when she was completing her education at

55
00:05:13,957 --> 00:05:19,879
an establishment at Hammersmith, and that on her being recalled home to nurse her father, he and

56
00:05:19,879 --> 00:05:25,398
she had confided their affection to the motherly Mrs. Whimple, by whom it had been fostered and

57
00:05:25,398 --> 00:05:31,639
regulated with equal kindness and discretion, ever since. It was understood that nothing

58
00:05:31,641 --> 00:05:37,237
of a tender nature could possibly be confided to old Barley, by reason of his being totally

59
00:05:37,237 --> 00:05:45,436
unequal to the consideration of any subject more psychological than Gout, Rum, and Purser’s stores.

60
00:05:45,438 --> 00:05:51,398
As we were thus conversing in a low tone while Old Barley’s sustained growl vibrated in the beam

61
00:05:51,398 --> 00:05:56,957
that crossed the ceiling, the room door opened, and a very pretty, slight, dark-eyed girl of

62
00:05:56,957 --> 00:06:02,641
twenty or so came in with a basket in her hand: whom Herbert tenderly relieved of the basket,

63
00:06:02,641 --> 00:06:09,519
and presented, blushing, as “Clara.” She really was a most charming girl, and might have passed

64
00:06:09,519 --> 00:06:16,358
for a captive fairy, whom that truculent Ogre, Old Barley, had pressed into his service.

65
00:06:16,358 --> 00:06:21,237
“Look here,” said Herbert, showing me the basket, with a compassionate and tender smile, after we

66
00:06:21,237 --> 00:06:27,277
had talked a little; “here’s poor Clara’s supper, served out every night. Here’s her allowance of

67
00:06:27,277 --> 00:06:33,519
bread, and here’s her slice of cheese, and here’s her rum,—which I drink. This is Mr. Barley’s

68
00:06:33,519 --> 00:06:40,597
breakfast for to-morrow, served out to be cooked. Two mutton-chops, three potatoes, some split peas,

69
00:06:40,597 --> 00:06:46,680
a little flour, two ounces of butter, a pinch of salt, and all this black pepper. It’s

70
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:52,957
stewed up together, and taken hot, and it’s a nice thing for the gout, I should think!”

71
00:06:52,957 --> 00:06:57,038
There was something so natural and winning in Clara’s resigned way of looking at these

72
00:06:57,038 --> 00:07:02,879
stores in detail, as Herbert pointed them out; and something so confiding, loving,

73
00:07:02,879 --> 00:07:08,879
and innocent in her modest manner of yielding herself to Herbert’s embracing arm; and something

74
00:07:08,879 --> 00:07:15,917
so gentle in her, so much needing protection on Mill Pond Bank, by Millstone Basin, and the Old

75
00:07:15,917 --> 00:07:22,078
Green Copper Rope-walk, with Old Barley growling in the beam,—that I would not have undone the

76
00:07:22,078 --> 00:07:27,839
engagement between her and Herbert for all the money in the pocket-book I had never opened.

77
00:07:27,839 --> 00:07:30,237
I was looking at her with pleasure and admiration,

78
00:07:30,879 --> 00:07:37,000
when suddenly the growl swelled into a roar again, and a frightful bumping noise was heard above,

79
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:41,199
as if a giant with a wooden leg were trying to bore it through the ceiling to come at

80
00:07:41,199 --> 00:07:49,116
us. Upon this Clara said to Herbert, “Papa wants me, darling!” and ran away.

81
00:07:49,116 --> 00:07:51,078
“There is an unconscionable old shark for

82
00:07:51,078 --> 00:07:56,199
you!” said Herbert. “What do you suppose he wants now, Handel?”

83
00:07:56,199 --> 00:08:00,480
“I don’t know,” said I. “Something to drink?”

84
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:06,078
“That’s it!” cried Herbert, as if I had made a guess of extraordinary merit. “He keeps his

85
00:08:06,078 --> 00:08:11,839
grog ready mixed in a little tub on the table. Wait a moment, and you’ll hear Clara lift him

86
00:08:11,839 --> 00:08:18,839
up to take some. There he goes!” Another roar, with a prolonged shake at the end.

87
00:08:18,839 --> 00:08:26,199
“Now,” said Herbert, as it was succeeded by silence, “he’s drinking. Now,” said Herbert,

88
00:08:26,199 --> 00:08:32,597
as the growl resounded in the beam once more, “he’s down again on his back!”

89
00:08:32,597 --> 00:08:38,597
Clara returned soon afterwards, and Herbert accompanied me upstairs to see our charge.

90
00:08:38,597 --> 00:08:43,277
As we passed Mr. Barley’s door, he was heard hoarsely muttering within,

91
00:08:43,277 --> 00:08:48,160
in a strain that rose and fell like wind, the following Refrain,

92
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:52,557
in which I substitute good wishes for something quite the reverse:—

93
00:08:52,559 --> 00:08:59,641
“Ahoy! Bless your eyes, here’s old Bill Barley. Here’s old Bill Barley, bless your eyes. Here’s

94
00:08:59,641 --> 00:09:05,160
old Bill Barley on the flat of his back, by the Lord. Lying on the flat of his back like

95
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:13,358
a drifting old dead flounder, here’s your old Bill Barley, bless your eyes. Ahoy! Bless you.”

96
00:09:13,358 --> 00:09:18,718
In this strain of consolation, Herbert informed me the invisible Barley would commune with

97
00:09:18,718 --> 00:09:25,758
himself by the day and night together; Often, while it was light, having, at the same time,

98
00:09:25,758 --> 00:09:32,116
one eye at a telescope which was fitted on his bed for the convenience of sweeping the river.

99
00:09:32,116 --> 00:09:37,319
In his two cabin rooms at the top of the house, which were fresh and airy, and in which Mr.

100
00:09:37,319 --> 00:09:44,718
Barley was less audible than below, I found Provis comfortably settled. He expressed no alarm, and

101
00:09:44,718 --> 00:09:50,718
seemed to feel none that was worth mentioning; but it struck me that he was softened,—indefinably,

102
00:09:50,718 --> 00:09:59,758
for I could not have said how, and could never afterwards recall how when I tried, but certainly.

103
00:09:59,758 --> 00:10:04,398
The opportunity that the day’s rest had given me for reflection had resulted in

104
00:10:04,398 --> 00:10:10,917
my fully determining to say nothing to him respecting Compeyson. For anything I knew,

105
00:10:10,917 --> 00:10:15,718
his animosity towards the man might otherwise lead to his seeking him out and rushing on his

106
00:10:15,718 --> 00:10:21,398
own destruction. Therefore, when Herbert and I sat down with him by his fire,

107
00:10:21,398 --> 00:10:27,160
I asked him first of all whether he relied on Wemmick’s judgment and sources of information?

108
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:32,917
“Ay, ay, dear boy!” he answered, with a grave nod, “Jaggers knows.”

109
00:10:32,917 --> 00:10:35,917
“Then, I have talked with Wemmick,” said I, “and have come to tell you

110
00:10:35,917 --> 00:10:42,519
what caution he gave me and what advice.” This I did accurately, with the reservation

111
00:10:42,519 --> 00:10:48,680
just mentioned; and I told him how Wemmick had heard, in Newgate prison (whether from officers or

112
00:10:48,680 --> 00:10:55,319
prisoners I could not say), that he was under some suspicion, and that my chambers had been watched;

113
00:10:55,319 --> 00:11:00,038
how Wemmick had recommended his keeping close for a time, and my keeping away from him;

114
00:11:00,796 --> 00:11:06,680
and what Wemmick had said about getting him abroad. I added, that of course,

115
00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:10,641
when the time came, I should go with him, or should follow close upon him,

116
00:11:10,641 --> 00:11:17,078
as might be safest in Wemmick’s judgment. What was to follow that I did not touch upon;

117
00:11:17,078 --> 00:11:22,078
neither, indeed, was I at all clear or comfortable about it in my own mind,

118
00:11:22,078 --> 00:11:30,160
now that I saw him in that softer condition, and in declared peril for my sake. As to altering my

119
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:37,199
way of living by enlarging my expenses, I put it to him whether in our present unsettled and

120
00:11:37,199 --> 00:11:43,116
difficult circumstances, it would not be simply ridiculous, if it were no worse?

121
00:11:43,116 --> 00:11:48,718
He could not deny this, and indeed was very reasonable throughout. His coming back was

122
00:11:48,718 --> 00:11:53,758
a venture, he said, and he had always known it to be a venture. He would do nothing to

123
00:11:53,758 --> 00:11:59,199
make it a desperate venture, and he had very little fear of his safety with such good help.

124
00:11:59,199 --> 00:12:04,480
Herbert, who had been looking at the fire and pondering, here said that something had come

125
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:09,199
into his thoughts arising out of Wemmick’s suggestion, which it might be worth while to

126
00:12:09,199 --> 00:12:14,678
pursue. “We are both good watermen, Handel, and could take him down the river ourselves

127
00:12:14,680 --> 00:12:21,038
when the right time comes. No boat would then be hired for the purpose, and no boatmen; that would

128
00:12:21,038 --> 00:12:27,199
save at least a chance of suspicion, and any chance is worth saving. Never mind the season;

129
00:12:27,199 --> 00:12:31,917
don’t you think it might be a good thing if you began at once to keep a boat at the Temple stairs,

130
00:12:31,917 --> 00:12:35,879
and were in the habit of rowing up and down the river? You fall into that habit,

131
00:12:35,879 --> 00:12:40,597
and then who notices or minds? Do it twenty or fifty times,

132
00:12:40,597 --> 00:12:45,237
and there is nothing special in your doing it the twenty-first or fifty-first.”

133
00:12:45,237 --> 00:12:50,160
I liked this scheme, and Provis was quite elated by it. We agreed that it should be

134
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:56,319
carried into execution, and that Provis should never recognise us if we came below Bridge,

135
00:12:56,319 --> 00:13:02,277
and rowed past Mill Pond Bank. But we further agreed that he should pull down the blind in

136
00:13:02,277 --> 00:13:09,078
that part of his window which gave upon the east, whenever he saw us and all was right.

137
00:13:09,078 --> 00:13:13,597
Our conference being now ended, and everything arranged, I rose to go;

138
00:13:13,597 --> 00:13:17,160
remarking to Herbert that he and I had better not go home together,

139
00:13:17,160 --> 00:13:21,680
and that I would take half an hour’s start of him. “I don’t like to leave you here,” I

140
00:13:21,680 --> 00:13:28,116
said to Provis, “though I cannot doubt your being safer here than near me. Good-bye!”

141
00:13:28,116 --> 00:13:31,917
“Dear boy,” he answered, clasping my hands,

142
00:13:31,917 --> 00:13:37,597
“I don’t know when we may meet again, and I don’t like good-bye. Say good-night!”

143
00:13:37,597 --> 00:13:41,078
“Good-night! Herbert will go regularly between us,

144
00:13:41,078 --> 00:13:48,000
and when the time comes you may be certain I shall be ready. Good-night, good-night!”

145
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:52,519
We thought it best that he should stay in his own rooms; and we left him on the landing outside

146
00:13:52,519 --> 00:13:59,078
his door, holding a light over the stair-rail to light us downstairs. Looking back at him,

147
00:13:59,078 --> 00:14:04,597
I thought of the first night of his return, when our positions were reversed, and when I

148
00:14:04,597 --> 00:14:10,718
little supposed my heart could ever be as heavy and anxious at parting from him as it was now.

149
00:14:10,718 --> 00:14:16,237
Old Barley was growling and swearing when we repassed his door, with no appearance of having

150
00:14:16,237 --> 00:14:23,237
ceased or of meaning to cease. When we got to the foot of the stairs, I asked Herbert whether he had

151
00:14:23,237 --> 00:14:30,358
preserved the name of Provis. He replied, certainly not, and that the lodger was Mr.

152
00:14:30,358 --> 00:14:38,639
Campbell. He also explained that the utmost known of Mr. Campbell there was, that he (Herbert) had

153
00:14:38,641 --> 00:14:44,597
Mr. Campbell consigned to him, and felt a strong personal interest in his being well cared for,

154
00:14:44,597 --> 00:14:50,680
and living a secluded life. So, when we went into the parlour where Mrs. Whimple and Clara

155
00:14:50,680 --> 00:14:58,519
were seated at work, I said nothing of my own interest in Mr. Campbell, but kept it to myself.

156
00:14:58,519 --> 00:15:03,917
When I had taken leave of the pretty, gentle, dark-eyed girl, and of the motherly woman who

157
00:15:03,917 --> 00:15:09,758
had not outlived her honest sympathy with a little affair of true love, I felt as if the Old Green

158
00:15:09,758 --> 00:15:16,116
Copper Rope-walk had grown quite a different place. Old Barley might be as old as the hills,

159
00:15:16,116 --> 00:15:21,519
and might swear like a whole field of troopers, but there were redeeming youth and trust and

160
00:15:21,519 --> 00:15:27,796
hope enough in Millstone Basin to fill it to overflowing. And then I thought of Estella,

161
00:15:27,796 --> 00:15:34,839
and of our parting, and went home very sadly. All things were as quiet in the Temple as ever

162
00:15:34,839 --> 00:15:40,839
I had seen them. The windows of the rooms on that side, lately occupied by Provis, were

163
00:15:40,839 --> 00:15:48,038
dark and still, and there was no lounger in Garden Court. I walked past the fountain twice or thrice

164
00:15:48,038 --> 00:15:54,879
before I descended the steps that were between me and my rooms, but I was quite alone. Herbert,

165
00:15:54,879 --> 00:16:00,480
coming to my bedside when he came in,—for I went straight to bed, dispirited and fatigued,—made

166
00:16:00,480 --> 00:16:06,838
the same report. Opening one of the windows after that, he looked out into the moonlight, and told

167
00:16:06,839 --> 00:16:13,237
me that the pavement was as solemnly empty as the pavement of any cathedral at that same hour.

168
00:16:13,237 --> 00:16:19,398
Next day I set myself to get the boat. It was soon done, and the boat was brought round to

169
00:16:19,398 --> 00:16:26,160
the Temple stairs, and lay where I could reach her within a minute or two. Then, I began to go

170
00:16:26,160 --> 00:16:34,116
out as for training and practice: sometimes alone, sometimes with Herbert. I was often out in cold,

171
00:16:34,116 --> 00:16:40,358
rain, and sleet, but nobody took much note of me after I had been out a few times. At first,

172
00:16:40,358 --> 00:16:46,078
I kept above Blackfriars Bridge; but as the hours of the tide changed, I took towards

173
00:16:46,078 --> 00:16:51,796
London Bridge. It was Old London Bridge in those days, and at certain states of the tide there was

174
00:16:51,796 --> 00:16:57,718
a race and fall of water there which gave it a bad reputation. But I knew well enough how

175
00:16:57,718 --> 00:17:03,519
to ‘shoot’ the bridge after seeing it done, and so began to row about among the shipping in the Pool,

176
00:17:03,519 --> 00:17:10,358
and down to Erith. The first time I passed Mill Pond Bank, Herbert and I were pulling a pair of

177
00:17:10,358 --> 00:17:18,038
oars; and, both in going and returning, we saw the blind towards the east come down. Herbert was

178
00:17:18,038 --> 00:17:22,641
rarely there less frequently than three times in a week, and he never brought me a single

179
00:17:22,641 --> 00:17:29,038
word of intelligence that was at all alarming. Still, I knew that there was cause for alarm,

180
00:17:29,038 --> 00:17:36,078
and I could not get rid of the notion of being watched. Once received, it is a haunting idea;

181
00:17:36,078 --> 00:17:42,557
how many undesigning persons I suspected of watching me, it would be hard to calculate.

182
00:17:42,559 --> 00:17:49,957
In short, I was always full of fears for the rash man who was in hiding. Herbert had sometimes said

183
00:17:49,957 --> 00:17:54,436
to me that he found it pleasant to stand at one of our windows after dark, when the

184
00:17:54,438 --> 00:18:00,559
tide was running down, and to think that it was flowing, with everything it bore, towards Clara.

185
00:18:01,319 --> 00:18:04,557
But I thought with dread that it was flowing towards Magwitch,

186
00:18:04,559 --> 00:18:10,358
and that any black mark on its surface might be his pursuers, going swiftly, silently, and surely,

187
00:18:10,358 --> 00:18:12,000
to take him. Chapter 47.
